{
  "number": 2,
  "title": "Music Fundamentals II: Minor Scales and Key Signatures, Melody, Timbre, Texture",
  "guide": {
    "summary": "Unit 2 covers minor scales and keys, intervals and their inversions, melodic features, transposition, timbre and instrument families, and musical texture.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Minor scales and keys",
        "body": "Natural minor follows W–H–W–W–H–W–W (scale degrees 1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7). Harmonic minor raises 7 to create a leading tone (A harmonic minor: G♯), producing an augmented second between 6 and 7. Melodic minor raises 6 and 7 ascending (A B C D E F♯ G♯ A) and uses natural minor descending. Relative keys share a key signature (C major / A minor; the relative minor is a minor third below). Parallel keys share a tonic (C major / C minor). In minor, the raised 7 is written as an accidental, not in the key signature."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Intervals",
        "body": "Intervals have a number (count letter names) and a quality: perfect (unisons, 4ths, 5ths, octaves), major or minor (2nds, 3rds, 6ths, 7ths), augmented or diminished. In a major scale, intervals above the tonic are major or perfect. Inversions: numbers sum to nine (3rd ↔ 6th), major ↔ minor, augmented ↔ diminished, perfect stays perfect. The tritone (augmented 4th or diminished 5th) spans three whole steps. Consonances: perfect unison, octave, fifth, and major/minor thirds and sixths; the perfect fourth is dissonant above the bass in common-practice harmony."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Melody, transposition, timbre, texture",
        "body": "Melodies may be conjunct (stepwise) or disjunct (leaps); they have range, contour, and climax, and are built from motives and sequences. Transposing instruments sound at a different pitch than written: B♭ clarinet and B♭ trumpet sound a major second lower; F horn sounds a perfect fifth lower; E♭ alto saxophone sounds a major sixth lower. Timbre is tone color (instrument families: strings, woodwinds, brass, percussion). Texture: monophony (single line), homophony (melody with accompaniment or chordal), polyphony (independent lines, e.g., imitation in a fugue), heterophony (simultaneous variants of one melody)."
      }
    ],
    "keyTakeaways": [
      "Natural, harmonic (raised 7), melodic (raised 6, 7 ascending) minor.",
      "Relative = same signature; parallel = same tonic.",
      "Interval inversions sum to 9; M↔m, A↔d.",
      "B♭ instruments sound M2 lower; F horn P5 lower.",
      "Textures: mono, homo, poly, hetero."
    ],
    "commonMistakes": [
      "Putting G♯ in the A minor key signature.",
      "Counting half steps but mislabeling interval numbers.",
      "Confusing relative and parallel keys.",
      "Transposing in the wrong direction."
    ],
    "examTips": [
      "Count letter names first, then quality.",
      "Listen for the raised leading tone in minor.",
      "Identify texture by counting independent lines."
    ],
    "cramSheet": "**Minor:** natural W H W W H W W · harmonic ↑7 · melodic ↑6 ↑7 ascending\n\n**Keys:** relative (m3 below) · parallel (same tonic)\n\n**Intervals:** P (1 4 5 8) · M/m (2 3 6 7) · inversion sums to 9 · tritone = A4/d5\n\n**Transposition:** B♭ = M2 lower · F horn = P5 lower · E♭ alto sax = M6 lower\n\n**Texture:** monophony · homophony · polyphony · heterophony"
  },
